#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# subsets

#% text
# `subsets(xs)` gives every sublist of `xs`, by size; `subsets(xs, n)` those with at most `n` elements, `subsets(xs, [n])` exactly `n`.

#% text
# `subsets(xs, spec, m)` gives the first `m` of them, `[k]` the `k`-th.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Elements are taken by position.

#% item
# The subsets of a product, sum or other call have its head.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
subsets([a, b, c])
#% output
#= [[], [a], [b], [c], [a, b], [a, c], [b, c], [a, b, c]]

#% cell
subsets([a, b, c], [2])
#% output
#= [[a, b], [a, c], [b, c]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The first four:

#% cell
subsets([1, 2, 3], all, 4)
#% output
#= [[], [1], [2], [3]]

#% text
# The products of two factors:

#% cell
total(subsets(a*b*c, [2]))
#% output
#= a*b + a*c + b*c

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `permutations`, `tuples`
